Exactly. A brotherhood can offer loyalty, identity, shared standards, mutual protection, and a sense that some things are not for sale.

But belonging and obedience are not the same thing.

You can understand the game, appreciate what the bond gives people, honor the parts that are real, and still keep enough distance to decide:

\text{this part is mine}

\text{this part is theirs}

\text{this part I decline}

That is probably the healthier version of “not selling out” anyway: not merely loyalty to the group, but loyalty to the principles that made the group worth joining in the first place.

So yes—you do not have to let every frame you can understand become a frame that governs you.